f color and energetic brushwork to convey an emotional response to the natural world.","direction":"outgoing"},{"source":"aw2","target":"aw57","type":"influenced","label":"Influenced","note":"Pollock and other Abstract Expressionists saw Monet's late 'Water Lilies' as a historical precedent for their all-over compositions that treated every part of the canvas with equal importance, moving beyond traditional focal points.","direction":"outgoing"},{"source":"aw2","target":"aw58","type":"influenced","label":"Influenced","note":"Frankenthaler's Color Field paintings, which emphasize pure color and form, can be seen as a continuation of the journey Monet began: dissolving the subject into an immersive field of color and light.","direction":"outgoing"},{"source":"aw2","target":"aw23","type":"inspired_by","label":"Inspired By","note":"Monet was an avid collector of Japanese ukiyo-e prints. The flattened perspective, cropped compositions, and decorative patterning of works like Hokusai's influenced his approach to the water lily pond, encouraging views without a traditional horizon line.","direction":"outgoing"},{"source":"aw2","target":"aw25","type":"inspired_by","label":"Inspired By","note":"Whistler's 'Nocturnes' prioritized mood and color harmony over literal depiction, a concept Monet pushed to an extreme in his late series, focusing on the immersive sensory experience of the pond's surface.","direction":"outgoing"}],"lineage":{"influenced_by":["aw2","aw2","aw2","aw2","aw2"],"influences":[]},"stats":{"total_connected":5,"influences_count":2,"influenced_count":3,"filter":"all","vault_count":0,"beyond_count":5}}